The attached image illustrates the local inflammatory response and cellular uptake of Nano-11 and ovalbumin (OVA) in skeletal muscle 24 hours post-injection. The top row shows significant infiltration of Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages (green) and MHCII+ cells (red), with intracellular Nano-11 (red) present in these cells when Nano-11+OVA was injected. In contrast, the bottom row, representing soluble OVA injection, shows few inflammatory cells and no detectable Nano-11, indicating Nano-11's role in recruiting immune cells and facilitating antigen uptake at the injection site.

The image can significantly contribute to vaccine development by providing visual evidence of Nano-11's ability to act as an effective adjuvant in vivo. It demonstrates the recruitment of antigen-presenting cells (monocytes, macrophages, MHCII+ cells) to the injection site and their efficient uptake of the Nano-11-antigen complex. This understanding of enhanced antigen delivery and local immune cell activation is crucial for designing and optimizing future vaccine formulations that elicit robust and targeted immune responses, particularly with biodegradable, plant-derived nanoparticles.